designs.exception_safety_model:
THREAT. An exception can land at any bytecode of the machinery, including:
- inside finally blocks;
- at a function's RESUME or its 'call' event, so the function never runs;
- at its 'return' event, so a completed call appears to raise;
- (3.11 adapter) at any c_call event, so CPython skips the C call.
Sources: signal-handler exceptions, KeyboardInterrupt, RecursionError, MemoryError, audit-hook refusals, a foreign profiler raising. The model assumes the GIL, which is why free-threaded builds are refused. Commits are single C operations: dict setitem, setdefault and pop with str, int or frame keys, set add and discard, one STORE_ATTR or STORE_GLOBAL, list.append. Their hashing and equality never run Python code.

INVARIANTS preserved by every prefix of every transition:
I1 (no over-credit). A hit is credited to opening o only if all of these hold: o's anchor is registered and on the hit frame's live f_back chain before the cut; o.core is a holder of the frame's mint; and id(code) is in core.by_code, which is emptied by exit's first store after its claim. A registered anchor whose frame died, from an interrupted close or an async exception between register and try, can never be on a live chain.
I2 (mint integrity). fn.__code__ is m.code only while m is registered with at least one holder whose token is live or which is active, except inside E4, X5 or _retire. In those cases the next reconciliation repairs it: holder before install, install last; restore before unregister, each step checked by identity.
I3 (reconciled registries). After any reconciliation, every registered mint has only live holders, and no mint without a live holder stays registered. The crash-sweep invariant C7 checks this with the faulted facades still alive.
I4 (single finalisation). Each opening is finalised exactly once, by o.fin.setdefault. Every cleanup is idempotent and unconditional, so exit or reconciliation completes a close interrupted at any point. After its core exits or is pruned, an opening is in no thread set and no anchor slot.
I5 (no blocking state held by the dead). The only exclusion is the robust mutex; a dead owner is stolen; a same-thread live owner refuses instead of waiting; a foreign live owner is bounded at 10 s (MACHINERY_BUSY). No threading lock exists, so a skipped release cannot hang anyone.
I6 (the envelope). A fault can change a record only within these bounds:
- counts ≤ the fault-free counts;
- each end ∈ {fault-free end, 'raised', 'open'};
- notes ⊆ fault-free notes ∪ {OPEN_AT_EXIT, PROFILER_LOST};
- no problem appears that the harness did not itself cause;
- otherwise record() refuses TRACE_INCOMPLETE or TRACE_ACTIVE.
A fault never causes over-credit, a hang, a mint left installed after reconciliation, a poisoned later trace, or a swallowed exception. The exception is the two documented user-code sites (import and module __getattr__), which convert to a chained UNRESOLVED.

PER-TRANSITION PREFIXES.
ENTER, when interrupted:
- before E4: nothing global has changed;
- inside E4: the core is a holder without 'active', and its entering token dies with the exception, so the next reconciliation prunes it and retires its mints;
- between the release and the 'active' commit: the same;
- after the commit, at the 'return' event: a zombie (see residuals).
EXIT, when interrupted:
- before the claim: a zombie;
- after the claim: record() refuses TRACE_INCOMPLETE; the exiting token dies, so the next reconciliation prunes the core, detaches its openings and retires its mints; partial X3 or X5 work is completed idempotently.
OPEN, when interrupted:
- before the append: nothing has changed;
- after the append, add or install: an unregistered or inert opening that exit finalises as 'open', and the thread's hook stays until exit discards the opening;
- after the anchor commit but before `try`: a dead anchor (L-ASYNC-EXC).
CLOSE, when interrupted:
- before the claim: exit claims it ('open', OPEN_AT_EXIT);
- after the claim: the cleanups are redone.
HOOK or CALLBACK: its only writes are single-key increments, clone flags and (3.11) the pend dict. An interrupt loses at most that count. On 3.11 CPython drops the hook, which gives the PROFILER_LOST note; on 3.12+ the tool stays.

RESIDUALS, disclosed:
- L-ZOMBIE: an exception at __exit__'s entry, or at __enter__'s 'return' event on a profiled thread, leaves an 'active' tracer. record() refuses TRACE_ACTIVE, and its mints stay installed (equal code) until the facade becomes unreachable and the next enter or exit reconciles. A stale anchor from a second fault on the same tracer can keep the facade alive through the frame's f_back chain.
- L-SIGNAL-311: on the 3.11 adapter, the hook makes every C call in USER code on a sectioned thread skippable. A `with lock:` release or an importlib module-lock release can be skipped. SIGNAL_TIMER refuses the detectable precondition; Ctrl-C (SIGINT) remains.
- GIL-only.

designs.semantic_mutation_gate:
GOAL. Prove the v5f exam detects WEAKENED rules, not only deleted refusals, with equivalence decided by execution, never by a reviewer. There are three frozen layers plus the existing refusal-deletion gate.

FROZEN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION. The v5f prereg lists sha256 for each of these, all written by the exam author, who is independent of the implementer:
- the exam runner;
- ref_v5f.py: a reference model, the executable spec written from this design; my prototype v5f_core.py is the pattern;
- rules_v5f.json: every numbered rule of the spec, including each crash-consistency ordering and claim;
- weakenings_v5f.py: catalog weakenings as exact-once text patches against ref_v5f.py, each carrying a rule id, operator family, witness id, the witness's spec outcome and its weakened outcome;
- witnesses_v5f.py;
- opmut_v5f.py: the operator generator for the real implementation;
- diffprobe_v5f.py: the differential probe with its normalizer and noise-mask procedure;
- crash_sweep_v5f.py: the lab's fault_injection_v5.py extended with the C1-C8 invariants and a background-tracer variant. On 3.12+ it injects through sys.monitoring INSTRUCTION events, because settrace opcode events on 3.12.3 skipped _enter, _enter_txn, _resolve and _open in the prototype, about 40% of points, even after warm-up;
- fuzz_v5f.py: the oracle fuzzer, with its grammar extended to nested tracers, shared and cross-named sections, clones, swaps, generator throw/close, cross-thread coroutine sections and gc.freeze.
The freeze commit also contains sm1_result.json. G_EXAM_FROZEN requires the freeze commit to be an ancestor of the first commit carrying the /3 tracer id.

SM1: CATALOG POWER, computed at freeze time on the reference only.
Admission is mechanical. A weakening W is admitted iff all three hold:
(i) its patch applies exactly once;
(ii) its witness scores the spec outcome on unpatched ref on 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13;
(iii) the witness's observable outcome under ref+W differs from ref's on at least one version.
Entries failing (iii) are listed as 'unwitnessed or equivalent' and dropped. Nobody rules on them.
Gate: the unpatched ref passes the exam, and the exam (mutation mode) detects 100% of admitted weakenings on every version they are admitted for. A weakening the exam misses is fixed BEFORE the freeze by adding its witness as a case.
Required catalog content:
- every row of the spec's mutation-audit table;
- all 63 round-4 census mutants re-targeted to ref where their rule still exists, and the 29 round-4 exam-hole kill shapes;
- crash-consistency weakenings M1-M10 from the prototype: a `with RLock` mutex; no reconcile; register before append; entering always live; exiting always live; non-idempotent detach; install before register; cross-thread discard skipped; claim after cleanup; credit not stopped at exit. M1-M8 are detected by the crash sweep. M9 is the pinned ENVELOPE-EQUIVALENT control and must NOT be admitted: its faulted outputs stay inside I6. M10 is killed by the exam's X71c-type case;
- one weakening per operator family below, applied inside each rule-tagged region of ref.

SM2: OPERATOR MUTANTS ON THE REAL IMPLEMENTATION, run after implementation; the procedure is frozen.
opmut applies these operators to every statement of the v5 region (the marker region plus the coverage methods of Experiment, check_metrics, score and _check_trace_shape):
- O1 delete a statement;
- O2 negate a test;
- O3 drop one operand of and/or;
- O4 is↔==, is not↔!=;
- O5 exact-type↔isinstance/issubclass;
- O6 in↔not in;
- O7 an int constant ±1;
- O8 swap two adjacent simple statements (commit order);
- O9 replace a `d.setdefault(k, v) is v` claim with an unconditional store (claim atomicity);
- O10 `for x in seq` → `seq[:1]` or `seq[-1:]` (all holders → one holder);
- O11 delete a break;
- O12 tuple rebuild → in-place mutation;
- O13 refusal deletion by coded LITERAL: every string literal containing [V5:CODE] anywhere in the region; its innermost enclosing statement becomes `pass`. This replaces statement-shape matching and closes all 8 blind shapes in mutation_gate_blindspots.json;
- O14 swap two sequential `if…: raise GateSpecError` blocks (step order).
Each mutant goes through these instruments in order, stopping at the first that detects it:
(a) the frozen exam in mutation mode;
(b) the frozen crash sweep, with a frozen stride;
(c) the frozen oracle fuzzer, with frozen seeds and N;
(d) the differential probe: the mutant against the unmutated implementation on the probe corpus (every exam case program, every fuzzer program, every crash-sweep point), comparing normalized observables: records, verdicts, refusal codes and messages, check_metrics output, registry snapshots, fixture __code__ identity, profiler and monitoring-tool state.
Fault-free corpus entries are compared by exact equality after a NOISE MASK. A field is masked iff it differs between two runs of the UNMUTATED implementation on that version; the mask is computed once and recorded. Faulted entries are not compared by equality: each output is checked against the frozen envelope I6. A mutant whose faulted outputs stay in the envelope is equivalent to the spec BY THE SPEC'S OWN TEXT; M9 is the worked example.
Classification:
- KILLED: (a), (b) or (c) detects it, or the mutant does not import;
- EXAM_HOLE: only (d) sees a difference, meaning an observable difference no frozen assertion reads;
- UNDISTINGUISHED: nothing sees it. It is reported with its diff as presumed equivalent and handed to the next red team; it is not a failure.
Gate: EXAM_HOLE = 0 on 3.11, 3.12 and 3.13; a clean baseline; at least one applied mutant per operator family. Counts of KILLED and UNDISTINGUISHED are reported.

SM3: unchanged from the v5e aux gates. The independent second implementation passes the frozen exam, matches the oracle, and gives identical normalized traces, now on 3.11-3.13 and on both adapters.

Why this proves detection of weakened rules: SM1 shows, before any implementation exists, that every catalogued weakening of every rule has an exam case that fails it. SM2 shows that for the actual code, every operator-generated weakening the frozen instruments can observe is asserted by the exam. The stated limit: the gate certifies detection of weakenings that some frozen instrument can observe; a weakening only a race or an untested version can expose is UNDISTINGUISHED, and it is reported, not hidden.

