PROTOTYPE. v5f_core.py (674 lines) is the full v5f state machine: robust mutex, _Txn tokens, reconciliation, claim-based open and close, per-thread sets, weakref abandonment, at-fork, monotone cut, gc.freeze CLONE_ALIVE, and both adapters (sys.monitoring on 3.12+, setprofile on 3.11, forced with V5F_ADAPTER=profile). v5f_core_profile_only.py is the earlier single-adapter snapshot.

1. t_func.py, 18 functional checks: 18/18 on 3.10.20, 3.11.15, 3.12.3 and 3.13.12, and with the profile adapter on 3.12. The checks: basic, sentinel, nested tracers, double exit, REENTRY recorded, X73 cut, run_async, an unstarted generator or closed coroutine not credited, a consumed generator {gen:3}, CLONE_CALLED, CODE_SWAPPED with the swap left in place, 4 concurrent threads exact, a chaining profiler kept, clean leftovers, code restored.

2. t_crash_sweep.py. An exception is injected at EVERY machinery bytecode the scenario executes: two tracers, nested and sequential sections, a coroutine section abandoned on a helper thread and detached by exit. Injection uses settrace opcodes on 3.10/3.11 and sys.monitoring INSTRUCTION events on 3.12+, because settrace missed about 40% of points on 3.12.3. Each point is checked against C1 no over-credit, C2 not swallowed, C3 a fresh trace on another thread is correct with no hang, C4 a background tracer sharing the mint keeps EXACT counts, C5 clean after the facades are dropped, C6 no unexpected refusal, C7 only live holders stay registered with the facades still alive, and C8 the record envelope.
Final dual-adapter results, both with and without the background tracer: 3.11 (setprofile) 7049 points, 0 failures, background tracer exact (16932 calls); 3.12 (monitoring) 6337 points, 0 failures; 3.13 (monitoring) 6283 points, 0 failures. Earlier runs of the profile adapter: 3.10 6686, 3.12 6325 and 3.13 6267 points, all 0 failures.
Results files: sweep_dual.txt, sweep_mon.txt, sweep_final2.txt.

3. run_mutants.py. The sweep has teeth: the variants below are all DETECTED on 3.11 and on 3.12 (both adapters):
- M1, the v5e-style `with RLock`, hangs;
- M2, no reconcile;
- M3, register before append;
- M4, entering always live;
- M5, non-idempotent detach;
- M6, install before register;
- M7, exiting always live (detected only after I added C7);
- M8, cross-thread discard skipped (detected only after I added the helper-thread section).
Two variants survive, as designed: M9 (claim after cleanup) is envelope-equivalent, and M10 (credit not stopped at exit) is an exam-case rule, not a crash rule.
Results: mutants_311.txt, mutants_312.txt, mut_final_311.txt, mut_final_312mon.txt.

4. t_sigalrm.py adapts the round-4 f12 repro: SIGALRM Timeout(Exception) across traced cycles nested in an outer tracer, with a worker-thread cycle every 50 cycles.
- 3.11: 116946 cycles, 31869 timeouts;
- 3.12: 233448 cycles, 26664 timeouts;
- 3.13: 227947 cycles, 26030 timeouts;
- in every case 0 mutex holds left, 0 worker hangs and clean leftovers, with code restored.
The same run surfaced three issues:
- 3.10 hangs workers inside CPython's importlib (import_module takes a module lock and its release is skipped), which supports refusing 3.10;
- a first `import asyncio.events` inside __enter__ hung 3.11 workers, so the import moved to module load;
- weakref.finalize callbacks were interrupted, so abandonment was replaced by a callback-free weakref.
Before that, the prototype's marks held frames, which kept the facade alive through f_back chains. That was found and fixed with frame-free _Txn tokens.

5. t_lsignal.py, a harness `with lock:` loop inside a section under SIGALRM, counting the user's lock left held:
- untraced: 0 leaks on every version;
- setprofile adapter: 3606 of 50385 timeouts (3.11), 3362 of 50651 (3.12), 3553 of 51461 (3.13);
- monitoring adapter: 0 of 51075 (3.12), 0 of 52456 (3.13).

6. t_lifecycle.py on 3.11-3.13:
- re-entry race: exactly one entry in 50 of 50 attempts, counts exact;
- MagicMock Handle._run: CUT_UNAVAILABLE with nothing minted;
- fork ProcessPoolExecutor jobs calling COV.run pass through with no hook, no anchors and original code; the parent trace has no problems;
- an audit hook refusing a __code__ write mid-mint: enter raises; a retry gives REENTRY; record() gives TRACE_INCOMPLETE; a later trace passes; leftovers are clean and code restored.

7. Probes (all identical across the versions listed):
- p_gen_progress.py (3.10-3.13): the f_lasti progress rule credits no unstarted, closed, thrown or bare-yield-closed resumption, and credits consumed generators and coroutines;
- p_mon_gen.py (3.12/3.13): throw() and close() fire only PY_THROW;
- p_mon.py: _getframe(1) inside a monitoring callback is the target frame with the true f_back chain, and a raising callback propagates while the tool stays active;
- p_cache_callee.py (3.10-3.13): gc.get_referents gives the real lru_cache callee, which the restamped __wrapped__ is not;
- p_prov.py (3.11-3.13): the co_filename rule refuses both stub-swap shapes and admits dataclass, wraps, wraps over a class wrapper, and the P1 .txt loader;
- p_resolve_c.py (3.11-3.13): descriptor reads bypass a strict __getattribute__, a metaclass __getattribute__, a property __dict__ and a module subclass;
- t_freeze.py (3.11-3.13): a frozen live clone gives CLONE_ALIVE, a benign freeze gives PASS, and a frozen live generator refuses (the disclosed over-block);
- t_cost.py: setprofile costs x11-17 on non-target code and x20-26 per hit at depth 11; monitoring costs x1.0 and x8.

Not run: the frozen v5e exam against the prototype. The API differs: /3, no _LOCK, _STOP or _THREADS globals, and the exam reads those. The lab's fault_injection_v5.py was not run against the prototype for the same reason.

